          equipment: '',
          dealing: 'Each player is dealt 5 cards (packets of 3 then 2).',
          hasPlaceholders: false,
        },
        rules: {
          objective: 'Each partnership aims to win at least three of the five tricks in each hand; the team that does so scores points (typically two unless the stake was raised), and the first team to reach 11 points wins the game.',
          gameplay: "1. Usually, there is NO obligation to follow suit or rank.\n2. **Exception (Trump Lead)**: If the 'Haube' (the principal trump) is led, others MUST play a trump if they have one.",
          keyRules: [
            'The three Criticals (Kritische Karten) are permanent trumps: King of Hearts (Maxl), Seven of Bells (Belli), Seven of Acorns (Spitz)',
            'Forehand chooses the Schlag (rank), dealer chooses the Farbe (suit)',
            'No obligation to follow suit except when the Haube is led',
            "Players can 'offer' to raise the stake during play",
          ],
          hasPlaceholders: false,
        },
        strategy: {
          basic: "Identify the 'Machine': If you or your partner holds all three Criticals, you have an unbreakable hand. Signal your partner immediately to ensure they don't 'offer' a raise until you are ready.",
          intermediate: 'The Signaling (Deuten): Use standard signals: Tongue to the side for the Maxl (KH), wink for the Belli (7B), and shoulder-shrug for the Spitz (7A). Coordination is key to winning without wasting high trumps on the same trick.',
          advanced: "Bluffing the Raise: If you win the first two tricks and have nothing left, 'offer' to raise the points to 3. If the opponents haven't coordinated their signals well, they may fold, giving you the win despite a weak hand.",
          tips: [
            "The 'Haube' (the card matching BOTH suit and rank chosen) is the most powerful non-critical card. Use it to force trumps out of your opponents' hands.",
            "In Tyrol, always check if the 'Weli' is being used as a critical or just a rank-filler; it changes the hierarchy significantly.",
          ],
          hasPlaceholders: false,
        },
